---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Lose disks and racks, and see what is lost" hint="3,000 objects of 64 MB sit on 16 racks of 16 disks. Pick how they are protected and where the pieces go, then run 12 rounds of failures with repair after each round. Every setting faces the same seeded sequence of failures.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>With the defaults (<b>6+3</b>, pieces <b>in one rack</b>, 3% disk and 3% rack loss) one rack dies in round 12 and takes about <b>225 objects</b> with it, since all 9 pieces of each were inside it. Nothing was wrong with the code, only with the placement.</li>
    <li>Switch to <b>spread across racks</b>: that same rack now takes one piece of an object, and the loss falls to about <b>8 objects</b>. Those come from disk failures that happen to pile up in a single round, which is the part that repair speed controls.</li>
    <li>Switch to <b>3 copies</b>: nothing is lost, but the overhead is 3.00x instead of 1.50x. Now raise disk loss to <b>8%</b>: 6+3 and 10+4 lose hundreds, 3 copies only about 30, and all of them move far more repair traffic. Erasure coding saves space only if disks are replaced quickly.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg osb" data-osb>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>Protection <select class="pg-in" data-scheme>
        <option value="rep3">3 copies (needs 1 of 3)</option>
        <option value="ec63" selected>6+3 erasure code (needs 6 of 9)</option>
        <option value="ec104">10+4 erasure code (needs 10 of 14)</option>
      </select></label>
      <label>Pieces are placed <select class="pg-in" data-place>
        <option value="spread">on different racks</option>
        <option value="one" selected>in one rack</option>
      </select></label>
      <label>Disks lost per round (%) <input type="range" min="0" max="10" step="0.5" value="3" data-pd /> <b data-pdval></b></label>
      <label>Racks lost per round (%) <input type="range" min="0" max="10" step="0.5" value="3" data-pr /> <b data-prval></b></label>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" data-b1><h3>Objects lost for good</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o1></span><span class="pg-note" data-n1></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b2><h3>Storage used</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o2></span><span class="pg-note" data-n2></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b3><h3>Repair traffic</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o3></span><span class="pg-note" data-n3></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b4><h3>Failures that happened</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o4></span><span class="pg-note" data-n4></span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-out" data-verdict aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p class="pg-note">Model: a round is one repair window. In each round every disk dies with the first probability and every rack, with all its disks, with the second. Objects that still have enough pieces are rebuilt onto new disks (in the same rack) before the next round, and objects with too few pieces are gone. Repairing a 6+3 or 10+4 object reads k pieces, which is a whole object of 64 MB, and writes each missing piece of 64 MB / k. Repairing a copy reads one copy and writes 64 MB per missing copy.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-osb]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = <T extends HTMLElement>(s: string) => root.querySelector<T>(s)!;
    const SCHEMES: Record<string, { k: number; n: number; name: string }> = { rep3: { k: 1, n: 3, name: '3 copies' }, ec63: { k: 6, n: 9, name: '6+3' }, ec104: { k: 10, n: 14, name: '10+4' } };
    const RACKS = 16; const DISKS = 16; const OBJECTS = 3000; const ROUNDS = 12; const SIZE = 64;
    const PLACE_SEED = 11; const FAIL_SEED = 2024; // separate streams for placement and for failures
    const MB_PER_GB = 1000;
    // mulberry32 PRNG
    const gen = (a: number) => () => { a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) | 0; let t = Math.imul(a ^ (a >>> 15), 1 | a); t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t; return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };
    const fmt = (n: number) => Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-US');

    function simulate(scheme: string, pDisk: number, pRack: number, spread: boolean) {
      const { k, n } = SCHEMES[scheme];
      const place = gen(PLACE_SEED); const fail = gen(FAIL_SEED); const ri = (m: number) => Math.floor(place() * m);
      const pick = (rack: number, used: number[]) => { let d: number; do d = rack * DISKS + ri(DISKS); while (used.includes(d)); return d; };
      const loc = new Int16Array(OBJECTS * n);
      for (let o = 0; o < OBJECTS; o++) {
        const used: number[] = []; const home = ri(RACKS); const racks: number[] = [];
        if (spread) { const all = Array.from({ length: RACKS }, (_, i) => i); for (let s = 0; s < n; s++) { const j = s + ri(RACKS - s); [all[s], all[j]] = [all[j], all[s]]; racks.push(all[s]); } }
        for (let s = 0; s < n; s++) { const d = pick(spread ? racks[s] : home, used); used.push(d); loc[o * n + s] = d; }
      }
      const alive = new Uint8Array(OBJECTS * n).fill(1); const gone = new Uint8Array(OBJECTS);
      let lost = 0; let rebuilt = 0; let readMB = 0; let writeMB = 0; let closest = n; let diskDeaths = 0; const rackEvents: string[] = [];
      for (let r = 0; r < ROUNDS; r++) {
        const dead = new Uint8Array(RACKS * DISKS);
        for (let a = 0; a < RACKS; a++) if (fail() < pRack) { rackEvents.push(`rack ${a + 1} in round ${r + 1}`); for (let d = 0; d < DISKS; d++) dead[a * DISKS + d] = 1; }
        for (let d = 0; d < RACKS * DISKS; d++) if (fail() < pDisk) { diskDeaths++; dead[d] = 1; }
        for (let o = 0; o < OBJECTS; o++) {
          if (gone[o]) continue;
          let left = 0;
          for (let s = 0; s < n; s++) { if (dead[loc[o * n + s]]) alive[o * n + s] = 0; left += alive[o * n + s]; }
          if (left === n) continue;
          if (left < k) { gone[o] = 1; lost++; continue; }
          closest = Math.min(closest, left - k);
          const used: number[] = []; for (let s = 0; s < n; s++) if (alive[o * n + s]) used.push(loc[o * n + s]);
          for (let s = 0; s < n; s++) if (!alive[o * n + s]) { const d = pick(Math.floor(loc[o * n + s] / DISKS), used); used.push(d); loc[o * n + s] = d; alive[o * n + s] = 1; rebuilt++; writeMB += SIZE / k; }
          readMB += SIZE;
        }
      }
      return { lost, rebuilt, readMB, writeMB, closest, diskDeaths, rackEvents, k, n };
    }

    let queued = false;
    function draw() {
      queued = false;
      const scheme = q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-scheme]').value; const spread = q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-place]').value === 'spread';
      const pd = Number(q<HTMLInputElement>('[data-pd]').value); const pr = Number(q<HTMLInputElement>('[data-pr]').value);
      q('[data-pdval]').textContent = `${pd}%`; q('[data-prval]').textContent = `${pr}%`;
      const s = simulate(scheme, pd / 100, pr / 100, spread); const sc = SCHEMES[scheme];
      const logical = (OBJECTS * SIZE) / MB_PER_GB; const stored = (logical * sc.n) / sc.k; const traffic = (s.readMB + s.writeMB) / MB_PER_GB;
      q('[data-o1]').textContent = `${fmt(s.lost)} of ${fmt(OBJECTS)}`;
      q('[data-n1]').textContent = `${((s.lost / OBJECTS) * 100).toFixed(1)}% of the objects`;
      q('[data-o2]').textContent = `${fmt(stored)} GB`;
      q('[data-n2]').textContent = `${(sc.n / sc.k).toFixed(2)}x for ${fmt(logical)} GB of objects`;
      q('[data-o3]').textContent = `${fmt(traffic)} GB`;
      q('[data-n3]').textContent = `${fmt(s.rebuilt)} pieces rebuilt: ${fmt(s.readMB / MB_PER_GB)} GB read, ${fmt(s.writeMB / MB_PER_GB)} GB written`;
      q('[data-o4]').textContent = `${fmt(s.diskDeaths)} disks`;
      q('[data-n4]').textContent = s.rackEvents.length ? `racks lost: ${s.rackEvents.join(', ')}` : 'no rack was lost';
      q<HTMLElement>('[data-b1]').style.setProperty('--c', s.lost === 0 ? 'var(--good)' : 'var(--bad)');
      q<HTMLElement>('[data-b2]').style.setProperty('--c', 'var(--accent)');
      q<HTMLElement>('[data-b3]').style.setProperty('--c', 'var(--accent)');
      q<HTMLElement>('[data-b4]').style.setProperty('--c', 'var(--accent)');
      const out = q('[data-verdict]'); out.classList.toggle('bad', s.lost > 0);
      const tail = s.closest < s.n ? (s.closest === 0 ? ' The closest call was an object left with exactly the minimum number of pieces: one more loss and it would have gone too.' : ` Every object that was damaged still kept at least ${s.closest} spare piece${s.closest === 1 ? '' : 's'}.`) : ' No object lost a piece.';
      if (s.lost === 0) out.textContent = `Nothing lost. ${sc.name} survived every round, using ${(sc.n / sc.k).toFixed(2)}x the space and ${fmt(traffic)} GB of repair traffic.${tail}`;
      else if (!spread && s.rackEvents.length) out.textContent = `${fmt(s.lost)} objects are gone, mostly because a whole rack died and every piece of those objects was inside it. The code could tolerate ${sc.n - sc.k} lost pieces, but one event took all ${sc.n}. Spread the pieces over racks and a rack failure costs one piece per object.${tail}`;
      else out.textContent = `${fmt(s.lost)} objects are gone: in some round more than ${sc.n - sc.k} of an object's ${sc.n} pieces died before repair could finish. Lower the failure rate (repair faster) or tolerate more losses (more parity, or more copies).${tail}`;
    }
    const schedule = () => { if (!queued) { queued = true; requestAnimationFrame(draw); } };
    root.addEventListener('input', schedule); root.addEventListener('change', schedule);
    draw();
  });
</script>
